---
// data-sources/index.astro
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../../config/site.js';
---

<Layout
  title="Data Sources"
  description="Transparent documentation of the data sources, update process, and verification methods behind TLD Directory."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
    <Breadcrumb items={[
      { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
      { label: 'Data Sources' }
    ]} />

    <article class="prose prose-ink max-w-none">
      <header class="mb-8">
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-ink-900">Data Sources & Methodology</h1>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink-600 text-lg">Where our data comes from, how it's verified, and how often it's updated.</p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>Primary sources</h2>
        <dl>
          <dt><strong>IANA Root Zone Database</strong></dt>
          <dd><a href="https://www.iana.org/domains/root/db" target="_blank" rel="noopener">https://www.iana.org/domains/root/db</a> — The authoritative table of all delegated TLDs, their types, and sponsoring organisations. Updated by IANA as delegations change.</dd>

          <dt><strong>IANA Delegated TLD List</strong></dt>
          <dd><a href="https://data.iana.org/TLD/tlds-alpha-by-domain.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">https://data.iana.org/TLD/tlds-alpha-by-domain.txt</a> — Plain-text list of all currently delegated TLD labels. Includes a version header with date. Updated weekly.</dd>

          <dt><strong>IANA Per-TLD Delegation Records</strong></dt>
          <dd><code>https://www.iana.org/domains/root/db/&#123;tld&#125;.html</code> — Individual pages for each TLD with registry information (WHOIS, RDAP, registration services URL), delegation history, and reference documents.</dd>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Registrar pricing sources</h2>
        <p>
          Price comparison data is kept fully separate from IANA metadata
          (<code>data/tlds.json</code> vs <code>data/prices.json</code>). Every price figure below is
          published by the source itself — we never estimate, convert, or invent prices. Sources
          without a value are shown as "Price unavailable".
        </p>
        <dl>
          <dt><strong>Porkbun API</strong> — official, no authentication</dt>
          <dd>
            <a href="https://porkbun.com/api/json/v3/documentation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Porkbun API v3 — pricing endpoint</a> —
            registration, renewal and transfer list prices in USD for every TLD Porkbun sells
            (~900). Documented as a public endpoint by Porkbun; fetched daily by our pipeline.
            Prices checked at fetch time, so these rows are the freshest on the site.
          </dd>

          <dt><strong>Pikkly open data</strong> — licence: CC BY 4.0</dt>
          <dd>
            <a href="https://pikkly.dev/data" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pikkly.dev/data</a> —
            daily-rebuilt JSON of first-year, renewal and transfer prices for 6 registrars
            (Porkbun, Cloudflare, Dynadot, Gandi, Hover, IONOS). Attribution:
            <em>"Price data by Pikkly"</em>. Rows sourced from dated community dumps use the dump
            date as their check date, not the rebuild time.
          </dd>

          <dt><strong>Open Domain Data — tld_pricing</strong> — licence: CC BY 4.0</dt>
          <dd>
            <a href="https://www.opendomaindata.org/datasets/tld-pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">opendomaindata.org</a> —
            monthly snapshot of register/renew/transfer prices normalised to USD across 7 registrars,
            including GoDaddy, Namecheap, Spaceship and Squarespace Domains. Citation:
            <em>Open Domain Data (2026). tld_pricing, opendomaindata.org</em>. Records carry their own
            check dates, which can be weeks old — anything past 30 days is labelled "stale".
          </dd>
        </dl>
        <p>
          We do not scrape registrar websites: these are an official public API and
          openly-licensed datasets, each fetched directly from the publisher. If a source requires
          credentials in the future, they are supplied via GitHub Actions secrets — never committed.
        </p>

        <h3>Pricing data pipeline</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Daily fetch</strong> — GitHub Actions runs <code>npm run sync:prices</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Validation</strong> — rows are rejected for unknown TLDs (anything not in the IANA catalog), invalid or out-of-range prices, missing USD currency, invalid URLs, and duplicates.</li>
          <li><strong>Failure guards</strong> — a source that errors, returns zero valid rows, or shrinks by more than 50% keeps its previous rows untouched; if every source fails, <code>data/prices.json</code> is left byte-for-byte unchanged and the run fails.</li>
          <li><strong>Merge</strong> — one offer per registrar+TLD; when sources overlap, the freshest check date wins.</li>
          <li><strong>Freshness</strong> — "verified" means the source's own check date is within 30 days; a rebuild can never re-date old prices.</li>
          <li><strong>Commit only on change</strong> — if the dataset is unchanged, nothing is committed, so no rebuild is triggered.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Supplemental sources (verified per-TLD)</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Registry websites</strong> — Official registration policy pages (linked from IANA's "URL for registration services").</li>
          <li><strong>ICANN Registry Agreements</strong> — For gTLDs, the base agreement and any amendments.</li>
          <li><strong>IANA Reports</strong> — Delegation/redelegation reports linked from per-TLD pages.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Editorial notes (registration restrictions, practical guidance) are researched from registry policies and cited explicitly. Unverified claims are marked as such.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Data processing pipeline</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Weekly sync</strong> — Automated script fetches both IANA sources every Monday (GitHub Actions).</li>
          <li><strong>Validation</strong> — Parsers reject malformed data, duplicates, or implausibly small datasets.</li>
          <li><strong>Merge</strong> — Delegated list (source of truth for "active" TLDs) joined with Root Zone DB rows (type, manager).</li>
          <li><strong>Detail enrichment</strong> — For TLDs without a manager in the DB table, the per-TLD delegation page is fetched for WHOIS/RDAP/URL/dates.</li>
          <li><strong>Normalisation</strong> — Types mapped to canonical values; IDN labels decoded to Unicode.</li>
          <li><strong>Change detection</strong> — Added/removed TLDs logged; meta.json updated with timestamps and counts.</li>
          <li><strong>Build</strong> — Astro SSG generates static HTML for all pages from the JSON dataset.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Data quality levels</h2>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Verified</strong></td><td>Type, manager, and delegation status confirmed against IANA sources.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Verified + Editorial</strong></td><td>Verified + human-reviewed editorial profile (description, restrictions, citations).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Incomplete</strong></td><td>Missing manager info (IANA shows "Not assigned") and no verified detail page data.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Known upstream change: IANA's 2026 page redesign</h2>
        <p>
          IANA redesigned both the Root Zone Database index page and the individual delegation
          records during 2026. Two things changed that affect this site:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Type taxonomy.</strong> The index page previously published six type values
            (generic, generic-restricted, country-code, sponsored, infrastructure, test). It now
            publishes three: <em>Generic</em>, <em>Country Code</em> and <em>Special Use</em>. As a
            result <code>.gov</code>, <code>.edu</code>, <code>.museum</code>, <code>.aero</code>,
            <code>.post</code>, <code>.biz</code>, <code>.name</code> and <code>.pro</code> now appear
            as Generic, and <code>.arpa</code> as Special Use. We follow what IANA publishes and
            record the change here instead of inferring the old labels.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Delegation status column.</strong> The index page now publishes a status per
            TLD (<em>Active</em>, <em>Retired</em>, <em>Reserved</em>, <em>Eligible</em>). The sync
            script cross-checks the count of <em>Active</em> rows against the authoritative delegated
            list and logs a warning if they diverge, but the delegated list still decides which
            records exist.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The parsers in <code>src/lib/iana.ts</code> support both the legacy and the current layouts,
          so a re-sync is stable regardless of which version IANA serves.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Update frequency</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>TLD metadata: automated weekly</strong> — Every Monday ~06:30 UTC (after IANA's typical update window).</li>
          <li><strong>Pricing: automated daily</strong> — GitHub Actions fetches all pricing sources every day; commits only when the dataset actually changed.</li>
          <li><strong>Manual trigger</strong> — Available via GitHub Actions workflow dispatch for both pipelines.</li>
          <li><strong>Emergency</strong> — If a major delegation change occurs, we can re-run immediately.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What we do NOT do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>We do <strong>not</strong> scrape registrar websites for pricing or availability — pricing comes from an official public API and openly-licensed datasets (see "Registrar pricing sources").</li>
          <li>We do <strong>not</strong> bypass authentication, anti-bot controls, or terms-of-use restrictions on any registrar's website.</li>
          <li>We do <strong>not</strong> guess prices: if a source doesn't publish a figure, we show "Price unavailable".</li>
          <li>We do <strong>not</strong> let affiliate relationships (none exist today) influence which offer is labelled cheapest — rankings use published prices only.</li>
          <li>We do <strong>not</strong> guess or infer registration rules — only cite verified sources.</li>
          <li>We do <strong>not</strong> track domain ownership, WHOIS history, or aftermarket data.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Licensing & attribution</h2>
        <p>IANA data is public domain / freely redistributable per <a href="https://www.iana.org/iana-legal" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IANA's legal notices</a>. Our compiled dataset, editorial content, and code are proprietary. You may link to our pages freely. For bulk data use, <a href="/contact/">contact us</a>.</p>
        <p>Third-party pricing datasets are reused under their own licences, with attribution shown here and on every detail page:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Pikkly</strong> — CC BY 4.0. <em>"Price data by Pikkly" — <a href="https://pikkly.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pikkly.dev</a></em>.</li>
          <li><strong>Open Domain Data</strong> — CC BY 4.0. <em>Open Domain Data (2026). tld_pricing, <a href="https://www.opendomaindata.org/datasets/tld-pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">opendomaindata.org</a></em>.</li>
          <li><strong>Porkbun</strong> — official public API, used per its published API documentation.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Verification & corrections</h2>
        <p>If you spot an error, please <a href="/contact/">contact us</a> with the TLD, the issue, and a source. We investigate and correct within one sync cycle.</p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>